    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. One thing we're seeing in the draft is that the clause mentions \"acts of God\" and \"unforeseeable disruptions beyond the control of the parties.\" If the disruption isn't a natural disaster but more of an unpredictable, chaotic event that just makes the premises unusable or disrupts supply chains, how would a court typically interpret the \"beyond control\" part? Is it a high bar to meet?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Beyond the control of the parties\" is a phrase that courts interpret with a degree of skepticism, often requiring a very high bar to meet. It generally means the event must be truly external, not just a difficult or expensive business challenge. If the disruption is due to supply chain chaos or unpredictable societal shifts, the court will look at whether the tenant took all reasonable steps to mitigate the issue. If the tenant could have found alternative suppliers or operational methods, the claim for force majeure might fail.",
      "source": "target",
