    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. It sounds like the burden of proof is really on the tenant to show they couldn't have prepared for it. Looking at the \"acts of God\" language—even if there isn't a specific natural disaster, would a series of highly unpredictable, erratic, or even \"mischievous\" disruptions to the local infrastructure (like strange, repeated technical failures or local disturbances) fall under that umbrella of an unforeseeable event?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Acts of God\" is traditionally reserved for truly natural occurrences—like typhoons or earthquakes—and generally does not encompass human-caused, albeit chaotic, infrastructure failures. To argue that repeated, unpredictable technical failures or local disturbances qualify, you'd need to argue they rise to the level of an *unforeseeable* and *unpreventable* systemic failure, rather than just poor maintenance or localized inconvenience. It's a very narrow interpretation, and a court might view these as operational risks the tenant should have factored into their business plan, not acts of nature.",
      "source": "target",
